 "text": {
  "en": {
   "headline": "Air temperature near the ground (Mar–May) changed at different rates in North Plain (example) and South Delta (example): the trend in the difference (North Plain (example) − South Delta (example)) is +0.15 °C per decade (95% range +0.09 °C to +0.21 °C).",
   "howMuch": [
    "North Plain (example): +0.38 °C per decade (95% range +0.33 °C to +0.45 °C). South Delta (example): +0.23 °C per decade (95% range +0.14 °C to +0.32 °C).",
    "Each place's own rate is shown beside the difference, never subtracted from it."
   ],
   "howSure": [
    "A secondary result: one of 12 season tests registered in advance; after correcting for them, p < 0.001.",
    "Mann–Kendall test allowing for year-to-year memory, before correction: p < 0.001.",
    "That is below the 0.05 line we set in advance."
   ],
   "checks": [
    "No missing years.",
    "No documented data change falls inside these years.",
    "No single year decides the answer.",
    "5 of 5 other windows agree."
   ],
   "practical": [
    "We decided in advance that a change of 0.10 °C per decade matters. The 95% range includes smaller changes, so it may or may not matter."
   ],
   "overturn": [
    "The answer leans most on 1999: without it, p < 0.001.",
    "The weakest of the other windows, 1983–2025, gives +0.14 °C per decade.",
    "If year-to-year memory were stronger or slower than our model assumes, the test would be too eager. Our check found no sign of that here."
   ],
   "notes": [
    "This comes from a model that blends many observations (a reanalysis). Models can shift when the observations they use change."
   ]
